Z||000000||Voyd||12-12-1999||01:45 PM||||you know my problem, now heres a little run down of what happened up till this point.<P>when i bought the car, like 2 days later the EGR valve went bust. it of course lit up the Check Engine light. didnt know how to check codes back then tho.  so i got the EGR valve fixed, along with a general Tune-up of the car. the car ran absolutely flawelessly.<P>i then leanred how to check for codes, and  that the codes stored had no priority and could be meaningless. so i pulled the codes and got a <BR>Code 33: MAP sensor<BR>Code 44: Lean Exhaust<P>since the EGR valve and tune-up the car has run flawlessly and the Check Engine light has never come on. i disconnected the battery and cleared the codes. i thought the previous codes were no longer valid and cuased by the bad EGR valve, if they were valid, they would have come up again. i also heard on here that resetting the computer was supposed to get it to learn the optimal values once more. obviously not...since my gas mileage is <B>horrible</B>. im talking 10 mpg here folks. city stop-n-go. i understand that city driving with the V6 is pretty much the worst case for gas milage, but 10mpg? cmon...thats harsh, and too expensive.<P>im running regular gas also. gas prices are too expensive here for the premium. but..if i  would hear from people here that Premium honest to god raises gas milage for the Fiero and gives better performance and its worth it, id put it in.<P>so...any help appreciated. im delighted with my car and its running flawlessly...if only i could get this problem fixed it would be perfect. also, if you could include the estimated prices of things to be replaced it would help me even more.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Voyd (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||8
Z||000001||Formula||12-12-1999||05:30 PM||||Mine takes around 8 gallons every 100 miles to fill. My EGR valve is waiting to be replaced and my check engine light doesn't work. The car runs perfect except for gas mileage. Mine is also stop and go V6. Maybe its supposed to be like that||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||12-12-1999||05:39 PM||||OK I drive in city traffic as well and for very short distancesabout a mile.  I likely use more gas starting the car than driving it.<P>I get about 12 mpg in my v6.   So I got concerned and devoted a weekend afternoon to test it on the highway.  Well I didnt get 30 mpg as some people boast about getting in their v6, but I remember getting about 23 to 25 mpg on a highway cruise at about 75 to 80 mph for the entire 8.5 gallon trip.<p>[This message has been edited by Greg Piet (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-12-1999||07:31 PM||||You guys don't mention what transmission you have. I have the 5-speed V-6 and usually get around 19-23 stop and go city, and 26-29 highway. Highway speeds for me are more like 65-70 though. Plus I drive real light on my car. One thing you can try is replacing the O2 sensor.  Sometimes they give a code, sometimes not. But if the O2 sensor is off, the engine will either run too lean(very bad) or too rich(bad). If it's running too rich mileage can get as bad as you guys are getting. It's worth a try.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||12-12-1999||10:02 PM||||I also get around 19-20 commuting to work on city streets, including some stop and go, and around 30 at normal freeway speeds in my 87 GT.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||12-12-1999||11:18 PM||||I measured fuel mileage on my V8 Batmobile this weekend for the first time. Half my driving was easy highway miles at about 60 MPH, 1/4 was city driving, and 1/4 was acting silly or showing off (hey, I actually had a woman riding with me for a while). I got about 18 MPG which I didn't think was too bad, considering the circumstances.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-13-1999||07:02 AM||||I'd also suggest looking at the O2 sensor.<P>High octane gasoline doesn't have any more power than regular gasoline.  Actually there are fewer BTUs per pound in high octane gas than in regular due to the additives that make the high octane.<P>The only advantage to high octane gas is it is harder to ignite and consequently less prone to knock.  This allows you to run higher compression and different timing without the damaging knock.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Voyd||12-13-1999||07:43 AM||||sorry for leaving it out, i have an Auto trans.<P>the o2 sensor huh? id like to change it, but my budget is running very low right now in the holiday season. i am guessing its an expensive part. can u physically see if it is bad? where is it located first of all? <P>oh, and right after i wrote this message i did some highway driving at about 130km/h for 1 hour to and back, and the gas mileage was about 25 mpg. <P>1 more thing, i thought these cars all had 10+ gallon tanks. you guys are saying 8.5? im confused.||||reg||1
