Z||000010||Clint||12-13-1999||02:29 PM||||I've had the same problems with my 4cyl.  Here's what i did...<BR>Gas mileage was 23, I finally got sick of it and crawled into the back of the car.  Found 2 sensor had been cut out and grounded.. Replaced and mileage went up to 30.  Few days later i started to get a 44 error and the car began to run rough (flooding out)  Gas mileage had dropped to 20.  Replaced the O2 sensor and it went up to 32, day or 2 later the problem came back with a vengence, gas mileage was down to 10! on my poor 5spd 4cyl.<P>I replaced the coolant sensor becuase i was still getting 44 error (the computer uses both to control the fuel mix).  My gas mileage is now in the 40's on the highway.  Yes i said 40's!!  I've driven over 100 miles now on just slightly over a 1/4 tank of gas.<P>hope this helps||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||12-13-1999||02:29 PM||||Thats where the funky Fiero fuel gauge and fuel pump comes into play.  Sometimes the fuel pickup tube is not laying in the bottom of the fuel tank, and you might have a gallon left, but the tube isnt down low enough to pick it up.  For this reason the fuel gauge isnt real accurate.  Its best to just deal with the fact that you have about 8.5 Gallons of functional gas, unless you want to drop your tank and lower the tube a bit, which is a bit o trouble.<P>As for milage, my 87 v6 auto gets 17-18 city and maybe 23 on the highway.  But I dont really ever get on it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Voyd||12-13-1999||05:02 PM||||clint- what were the 2 other sensors named that u changed first? also, where is the o2 sensor located if u dont mind?<P>djrice - about the funky fuel gauge, my gauge drops pretty considerably when going up/down hills, and accelerating hard. after i back off the throttle it creeps back to where it was. i know the fiero has a non standard gas tank, but its kinda weird to see a fuel gauge constantly moving down/up in real time like that   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Voyd (edited 12-13-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||12-13-1999||06:49 PM||||On the v-6 the O2 sensor is on the end of the y-pipe near the bottom.  You'll know it when you see a purple wire coming from it. Don't know where it is on the 4-cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Clint||12-13-1999||08:58 PM||||I replaced the Coolant sensor (on the top of the head, not the temperature sending unit)and what turned out to be the fan switch. The fan switch was grounded to the frame before.<P>The O2 sensor on the 4 is straight back on the exhaust header.  REAL EASY to get to.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Standard||12-14-1999||01:47 AM||||Voyd and DJRice,<BR>My fuel gauge does exactly the same thing... I'm always watching it move around.  I've also found that I usually have around 3 gallons left when it starts to touch that shaded/warning area.  I usually fill it up before that, tho.. ever since I ran out of gas right and rolled to a stop right in front of my house...<P>oh yeah, I saw in a pontiac brochure someplace on the net that the 84 Fiero was rated at 50 mpg highway. <p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 12-14-1999).]||||reg||1
